into carriers during the synthesis of supported catalysts.[18-19] As reported by Xiao and his coworkers, M/Sn-TiO2 $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{u}$ , Ru, Pt, Ni) catalysts present high selectivity in nitroarene hydrogenation due to the promotion effect of Sn sites in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ carrier.[20] It is not easy to determine the structure of metal promoters in these carriers as they are randomly distributed and are easily destroyed in the formation process of active nanoparticles (NPs) under reductive conditions.[21] Thirdly, metal promoters as components are introduced into NPs such as bimetallic or even polymetallic catalysts.[22-23] Kyriakou’s group prepares PtCu bimetallic NPs with high activity and remarkable selectivity in furfural hydrogenation reaction because of the regulation effect of Cu promoters.[24] Similar to metal promoters in carriers, this approach also faces the problem of defining the promoter structure in most of these catalysts.[25] Hence, it is still challenging yet meaningful to clear the real action mechanism behind metal promoters in the catalytic field, mainly due to their complex existing status. In addition, along with the development of theoretical simulation in recent years, researchers have been committed to pursuing structurally ordered metal promoter models to further explore the mechanism behind metal promoters.
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) feature ordered periodic networks and high porosity, which have been considered as potential candidates for building the structurally ordered metal promoter models.[26-29] Firstly, MOFs can support monodispersed metal NPs through impregnation reduction or in-situ encapsulation strategy, which have been applied in a variety of catalytic reactions.[30-38] Recently, Li’s group proposes a locking effect to load ultrafine metal NPs into MOFs[39] and the as-synthesized Au@L-ZIF-90 catalyst exhibits good selectivity and high stability in selective hydrogenation of 3-nitrophenylacetylene. Secondly, MOFs possess rich physico-chemical microenvironments originated from their inherent modular structures: tunable metal clusters and organic linkers. The organic linkers of MOFs can be flexibly designed with special functional groups, such as amino and carboxyl, showcasing certain chelating capability. These chelating sites are easy to graft single metal sites onto their structures,[40-45] and the grafting metal sites can still maintain their partial structure as metal salts or metal complexes,[46-47] which provides convenience for the
establishment of structurally ordered metal promoter models. Therefore, we assume that if metal promoters are reasonably introduced into MOF supported metal catalysts, they will not only enrich the type and function of MOF supported metal catalysts, but also provide structurally ordered metal promoters, which is beneficial to reveal the action mechanism behind the metal promoters in the catalytic reactions.
Herein, the free metal promoters are successfully incorporated into the chelating sites of MOF supported catalysts through a flexible targeted post-modification strategy (Figure 1), which provides a feasible research platform to elucidate the action mechanism behind metal promoters through simplifying the catalytic system. For example, the selectivity of Pd/bpyUiO catalyst in 4-nitrostyrene (NS) hydrogenation is significantly improved by anchoring Cu promoters onto the ligand of bpy-UiO (Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu). The Cu promoters show a clear and homogeneous structure, that is, single-site metal promoters anchoring onto the 2,2'- bipyridine- $^{\cdot5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid (bpydc) ligands of bpy-UiO with chlorine anions as counterions. As revealed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements, diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) and density functional theory (DFT) calculations, these Cu promoters enhance the interaction between MOF carriers and Pd NPs and weaken their hydrogenation ability on nitro groups. Together with the preferential desorption from Pd surface, the prepared Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalysts exhibit high selectivity toward 4-nitrophenylethane. Moreover, diverse transition metal and noble metal promoters, a variety of carriers (MOFs, COFs, etc.) and different active metal NPs (Pt NPs, Pd NPs, etc.) endow the targeted post-modification strategy with great universality and flexibility. Besides providing a new tool for the design and synthesis of high-efficiency catalysts, this strategy also offers an effective platform for elucidating the structure and mechanism behind promoters in the catalytic process.
