promoters.[51-52] After introducing $\mathrm{Cu}$ promoters, the obtained Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalyst shows increased metal-N-pyridine peak intensity at $399.5\mathrm{eV}$ in the N 1s binding energy but consistent O $1s$ and $\displaystyle Z\mathbf{r}3d$ binding energies to Pd/bpy-UiO catalyst (Figure 2f–h), reflecting Cu promoters are anchored onto the double nitrogen sites of bpydc ligands. Besides, chlorine element is observed in Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu sample but not in Pd/bpy-UiO as revealed by XPS measurements (Figure 2i) and TEM elemental mapping images (Figure S8). According to a previous literature,[49] the retained chlorine anions from metal chloride precursors are balanced to the electronegativity of MOF channels. In a word, single-site Cu promoters are anchored onto the double nitrogen sites of bpydc ligands with chlorine anions as counterions (Figure 2a).
## 2.2 Selective hydrogenation of 4-nitrostyrene
Selective hydrogenation of NS was selected to explore the catalytic performance of the prepared Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalysts. Both the nitro groups and vinyl groups of NS could respectively hydrogenate to generate 4-vinylaniline (AS) and 4-nitrophenylethane (EN), followed by hydrogenating to produce 4-ethylaniline (EA) (Figure 3a). Therefore, restraining the excessive hydrogenation in catalytic process plays a crucial role to obtain high selectivity toward intermediate products. Though Pd/bpy-UiO catalyst possesses high selectivity $(>89\%)$ toward EN upon the complete transformation of NS at $80\mathrm{min}$ , the yielded EN is quickly transformed into EA, resulting in low selectivity $(54.7\%)$ at $160\mathrm{min}$ (Figure S9). The significantly declined selectivity with prolonging the reaction time hinders the practical application of Pd/bpy-UiO catalyst. Herein, the excessive hydrogenation is mainly attributed to the high activity of Pd NPs, thus activity regulation of Pd NPs becoming critical to selectivity improvement. As reported by previous literatures, many kinds of metal sites (Fe, Cu, Co, Zn, etc.) as promoters can regulate the catalytic performance of metal NPs, among which Cu promoters usually display stronger regulation than others.[6,53] Accordingly, Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalyst with structurally ordered $\mathrm{Cu}$ promoters is selected to catalyze NS hydrogenation. It shows high selectivity $(>99\%)$ ) toward EN even after reacting $24\mathrm{h}$ (Figure
3b and Figure S9c). The enhanced selectivity is mainly ascribed to the lower activity of Pd NPs in nitro hydrogenation regulated by $\mathrm{Cu}$ promoters (Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ and Figure S10).
In order to further demonstrate the effect of $\mathrm{Cu}$ promoters on the selectivity and activity, the contents of $\mathrm{Cu}$ promoters in ${\mathrm{Pd}}/$ bpy-UiO-Cu were further adjusted. Specifically, the catalytic activity of Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu in NS hydrogenation reaction decreases with the increased contents of $\mathrm{Cu}$ promoters (Figure 3c and Figure S11). Notably, Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu (25 mM) with excess Cu promoters exhibits distinctly decreased yield of EN $(0.81\%)$ ) compared to the $100\%$ yield shown by Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu (5 mM). Similarly, Pd/HKUST-1 also shows poor activity in the hydrogenation reaction (yield of EN at $24\mathrm{h}$ is only $40.2\%$ ) because HKUST-1 crystal assembled by Cu ions with trimesic acid ligands possesses abundant $\mathrm{Cu}$ promoters (Figure 3d and Figure S12−13). In addition, Pd/UiO-67-Cu, which is prepared by immersing Pd/UiO-67 in $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ solution and washing it several times, shows no selectivity toward EN (Figure 3e). Since bpy-UiO presents similar crystal structure to UiO-67 except for double nitrogen sites of ligand (Figure S14a−b), chelation sites are considered important to anchor Cu promoters. To prove the structure stability of anchored Cu promoters during catalytic process, a control experiment was conducted by utilizing the mixture of Pd/bpy-UiO and bpyUiO-Cu as catalyst in NS hydrogenation. After reacting for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , the NS is totally converted into EN $(2.47\%)$ and EA $(97.53\%)$ (Figure 3f). The low selectivity signifies that Cu promoters are stably anchored on bpy-UiO and the released trace copper ions exert negligible impact on the selectivity toward EN. In addition, leaching experiment, recycling experiment and $\mathrm{Cu}$ content measurements reveal that Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalyst possesses high structural stability and property stability (Figure S15−17, Table S1). Therefore, the improved selectivity of Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalyst is derived from the structurally ordered metal promoters anchored onto bpydc ligands.
