lower values $(-0.3\mathrm{eV})$ than that of bpy-UiO- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{c}_{\mathrm{u}}$ (934.6 eV for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $932.6\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}^{+/0}$ ) (Figure 4c). In comparison with $\mathrm{Pd}/$ bpy-UiO, the $\mathrm{Cu}$ species in ${\mathrm{Pd}}/$ bpy-UiO-Cu exhibites higher ratio of $\mathrm{Cu}^{+/0}$ species after the combination with Pd NPs. These differences reflect partial electron transfer from Pd NPs to single-site $\mathrm{Cu}$ promoters, which is well matched with the red-shift betwwen Pd/bpy-UiO $(2038\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) and Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu $(2043~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ ) in CODRIFTS spectra (Figure S18). The electronic status of Pd NPs might be regulated by the inserted Cu promoters, which corresponds to the hydrogenation ability on nitro group of Pd NPs. In addition, after being mixed with tungsten oxides and exposed to hydrogen atmosphere for 5 min, the mixture containing Pd/bpy-UiO shows obvious color change, while the mixture containing Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu exhibits negligible color change (Figure 4f). The above results demonstrate that the Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalyst possesses lower hydrogen cracking capacity than Pd/bpy-UiO. Hence, the single-site Cu promoters decrease the hydrogenation ability of Pd NPs and further improve their selectivity in NS hydrogenation reaction through controlling electronic transfer between Pd NPs and Cu promoters.
## 2.4. Theoretical calculation
To further elucidate the action mechanism behind Cu promoters in NS hydrogenation catalyzed by Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu, DFT was utilized to simulate the interactions between Pd NPs and bpy-UiO or bpy-UiO-Cu carriers. The crystallographic information files (CIFs) of bpyUiO and bpy-UiO-Cu carriers are obtained from previous literatures.[54-55] To optimize the calculative procedure, Pd13 cluster constituted by $13\mathrm{Pd}$ atoms and MOF unit cell composed by 4 metal clusters and 6 bpydc ligands, were selected to establish theoretical calculation models, including Pd13/bpy-UiO and Pd13/bpy-UiO-Cu (Figure S19). It is worth noting that the simulated binding energies between $\mathrm{Pd}_{13}$ cluster and MOF carriers are respectively $-5.99$ eV (Pd13/bpy-UiO) and $-7.12\mathrm{eV}$ (Pd13/bpy-UiO-Cu) (Figure 4d). Obviously, the inserted Cu promoters could enhance the interaction between Pd NPs and MOF carriers and further enhance the electron transfer from Pd NPs to MOF carriers, which is also in line with the XPS results. As a result, Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalyst presents lower hydrogenation ability on nitro groups than pristine Pd/bpy-UiO catalyst.
Furthermore, the adsorption energies of NS molecule and corresponding hydrogenation product (EN, AS and EA molecules) on the surface of Pd NPs were also calculated. The four kinds of substrate molecules are adsorbed on the surface of Pd36 $(6\times6)$ matrix (Figure S20) and the simulated adsorption energies are $-0.24\ \mathrm{eV}$ (NS), $-0.32\mathrm{eV}$ (EN), $-0.37\mathrm{eV}$ (EA) and $-1.19\mathrm{eV}$ (AS), respectively (Figure 4e). Compared to AS and EA molecules, EN molecule shows the highest adsorption energy, which means the generated EN molecule by NS hydrogenation is easy to be desorbed from the surface of Pd NPs. Meanwhile, since there is no activity in hydrogenation of nitro group, Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu catalyst presents higher selectivity toward EN in NS hydrogenation than pristine Pd/bpy-UiO catalyst.
## 2.5 Universality of the targeted post-modification strategy
