## WILEY-VCH
Due to the flexibility of the targeted post-modification strategy, a variety of metal promoters, carriers and metal NPs can be matched to prepare supported metal catalysts with structurally ordered metal promoters. Firstly, the strong bpydc ligands could anchor various transition metal promoters, including not only traditional non-noble metal promoters $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{V},\mathbf{Cr},\mathbf{Mn}.$ ， Fe, Co, Ni and Mo), but also noble metal promoters ( $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{R}\mathbf{u}$ , Rh, Au and $\mathrm{Pt}$ ) and even bimetal promoters (Figure ${5}\mathrm{a}{-}\mathrm{o}$ ). All the prepared Pd/bpy-UiO-M catalysts possess similar structure to that of pristine Pd/bpy-UiO catalyst as testified by XRD measurements (Figure S21−22) and consistent metal promoter structure as proved by TEM elemental mapping images (Figure S23−34) and XPS measurements (Figure S35−42). Notably, these kinds of asprepared Pd/bpy-UiO-M catalysts all show superior selectivity to pristine Pd/bpy-UiO catalysts in NS hydrogenation (Figure S43−45), mainly due to the enhanced interaction between MOF carriers and Pd NPs (Figure S46). Generally, the noble metal ions are hard to be assembled into MOFs as metal nodes,[56] while Pd/bpy-UiO-M ( $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{R}\mathbf{u}$ , Rh, Au and Pt) nanomaterials with noble metal promoters are prepared by the targeted post-modification strategy (Figure $5\mathrm{i-m}$ ). Remarkably, bimetal promoters (Fe and $\mathrm{Cu}$ ) could be simultaneously introduced into Pd/bpy-UiO (Figure $5\mathrm{n}{-}\mathrm{o}$ ), and the as-prepared Pd/bpy-UiO-FeCu shows different catalytic properties to Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu or $\mathrm{Pd}/$ bpy-UiO-Fe (Figure S47), which further implies the flexibility of the targeted post-modification strategy.
Moreover, the targeted post-modification strategy can be extended to other carriers with chelating sites, including original and grafted chelating sites. In addition to bpy-UiO, another four kinds of representative carriers were adopted to explore the universality of this strategy, including MOF-808-EDTA, UiO-66-sal, Tp-Azo-COF and polydopamine. Among them, TpAzo-COF and polydopamine possess chelating sites in their initial structure,[57-58] while the chelating sites of UiO-66-sal and MOF-808-EDTA are formed through post-modification process (Figure S48−49).[59-60] Furthermore, the extended metal NPs (Pt NPs and Pd NPs) are respectively loaded on selected carriers to further expand the variety of as-prepared catalysts. Hence, four kinds of supported catalysts with structurally ordered metal promoters including Pt/MOF-808-EDTA-Cu (Figure 5p), Pt/UiO-66-sal-Co (Figure 5q), Pd/Tp-Azo-COF-Cu
(Figure 5r and Figure S50) and Pd/polydopamine-Cu (Fig. 5s and Figure S51) are successfully prepared, as demonstrated by XRD measurements (Figure S52−54), Fourier transform-infrared spectra (Figure S55) and HRTEM images (Figure $5\mathrm{p-s}^{\cdot}$ ). Indeed, the diversity of carriers (MOFs, COFs, polymers, etc.), metal promoters (transition metal ions and noble metal ions) and active metal NPs (Pd, Pt, etc.) endow the targeted postmodification strategy with attractive universality and creativity.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, we develop a targeted post-modification strategy to construct structurally ordered metal promoters through anchoring metal promoters onto supported metal catalysts, which helps to reveal the action mechanism behind metal promoters during catalytic process. Typically, single-site Cu promoters featuring a homogeneous and clear structure are anchored onto the N sites of bpydc linkers to prepare Pd/bpy-UiO-Cu, which presents higher selectivity than pristine Pd/bpy-UiO in NS hydrogenation. As proved by DFT calculations and comprehensive characterizations, the prior desorption of generated EN molecules from Pd NPs, together with the enhanced interactions between Pd NPs and MOF carriers by metal promoters, could weaken their hydrogenation capacity on nitro and contribute to high selectivity toward EN. Moreover, the diverse integrations of metal NPs (Pd NPs, Pt NP, etc.), metal promoters (noble metals, and transition metals) and carriers (MOFs, COFs, etc.) endow the targeted post-modification strategy with appealing universality and flexibility. In short, as a facile, flexible and universal methodology, this strategy provides insightful guidance to synthesize high-efficiency catalysts with structurally ordered metal promoters and facilitates the investigation of action mechanism behind promoters, which will advance the development in catalysis.
